# Warren — environment configuration.
#
# Casual installs never open this file. `warren up` collects the credentials
# it needs interactively and stores them under ~/.warren/ (mode 0600). Read
# docs/quickstart.md for that path. Real environment variables always win over
# the wizard's stored file.
#
# This file is the operator reference. Copy it to `.env` (`cp .env.example
# .env`) before `docker compose up`. Variables are warren-namespaced
# (`WARREN_*`) except for cross-tool ones warren consumes verbatim
# (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GITHUB_TOKEN). Read docs/credentials.md for the model
# and GitHub credential paths in full.

# ---------- first-run credentials ----------

# Operator bearer for protected API and UI actions. Under the default token
# auth mode, leave this blank on first boot to let warren mint a token, persist
# it under WARREN_DATA_DIR, and print it once as `mintedOperatorToken` in the
# boot log. Set an explicit value when the deployment supplies its own secret;
# that value always wins. Health/version probes, bootstrap registration, and
# static UI assets have their documented unauthenticated posture, while
# operator and mutation surfaces require this bearer.
#
# The operator token also seeds run-scoped callback tokens and signed preview
# cookies whenever previews are enabled, in path or subdomain mode. `warren
# doctor` warns when an explicit token matches a placeholder or is too short.
WARREN_API_TOKEN=

# At least one provider/SCM token reaches the agent so it can call its model
# API and operate on the project repo. Both are optional at warren boot and
# only required when a run or forge operation uses them.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is forwarded into the sandbox by warren's claude-code
# runtime profile, which default-allows ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, and CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
# (src/runtime/local/profile.ts).
#
# NOTE on cost caps (warren-f3c3): warren computes costUsd at API prices
# regardless of credential, and maxCostUsd always enforces against that
# number — it is a runaway brake on estimated usage, not a bill. When only
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN is set (subscription auth, no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY),
# runs are stamped costBasis=subscription_estimate and the UI shows the cost
# as an estimate; the cap still applies to that estimate.
#
# Multi-provider agents (e.g. the pi runtime) read additional provider keys
# from the container env. Set whichever providers your agents use; warren's
# per-runtime env allowlist forwards them into the sandbox. The supervisor
# inherits container env into the warren process by default, so just
# adding a variable here is enough — no docker-compose change required.
#
# GITHUB_TOKEN is the static-PAT forge credential (WARREN_FORGE=github). Git
# network ops (reap-time `git push`, project clones, refreshes) authenticate
# per-spawn via minted credentials, never via a global gitconfig rewrite.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Optional provider keys for multi-provider runtimes (warren-f8c0). Warren's
# runtime profiles forward these into the sandbox when set; unset keys are
# silently skipped.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# PI_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#
# The canonical provider → env-key mapping lives in src/core/providers.ts
# (warren-fb8d). On the k8s topology each provider's canonical key is sourced
# from a per-provider Secret (`warren-<provider>-key` / `api-key`) instead of
# the process env — see deploy/k8s/base/secrets.yaml.

# ---------- optional: published image tag (compose only) ----------

# Which `ghcr.io/jayminwest/warren` tag docker-compose.yml pulls
# (warren-26be). Compose reads this at interpolation time; warren itself
# never sees it. `latest` tracks main — pin a release (`v0.10.4`) or a
# moving minor (`0.10`) if you'd rather upgrade deliberately.
# WARREN_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# ---------- optional: auth provider (warren-851b) ----------

# Which AuthProvider protects the API. Resolved ONCE at boot; an
# unrecognized value refuses the boot rather than falling back.
#
#   token   (default) every route except /healthz requires the
#           WARREN_API_TOKEN bearer token. Leave it unset for this.
#   public  READ-ONLY ANONYMOUS ACCESS IS ON. A caller with no
#           Authorization header is admitted as an anonymous actor
#           holding the `readPublic` capability: it can read the public
#           projection of runs, projects and agents — including run
#           prompts and per-run cost — on EVERY project registered on
#           this instance. Mutations, operator-only surfaces and
#           diagnostics stay closed. Only turn this on for an instance
#           whose registered repos are all public and whose run prompts
#           you are happy to publish.
#
# WARREN_API_TOKEN is required in BOTH modes — `public` widens who may
# read, it never removes the operator's way in. A token holder still
# gets the full-capability operator actor, so one deployment serves both
# spectators and you. `--no-auth` (loopback dev) still wins over this.
# WARREN_AUTH=token

# ---------- required WHEN WARREN_AUTH=public: allowlist (warren-ce9b/1841) ----------

# Comma-separated list of what this public instance may hold. Each entry is
# EITHER a bare GitHub owner (my-org) or one specific repo
# (some-owner/some-repo). Both shapes may appear in the same list.
# Read ONLY under WARREN_AUTH=public; token mode ignores it entirely.
#
# This is the structural half of the public posture: the per-response
# field redaction is an allowlist per body, this is the guarantee that
# there is nothing private on the instance for a redaction miss to leak.
# Two enforcement points:
#
#   boot            every already-registered project must match an entry,
#                   or warren refuses to start and names the offenders.
#                   Delete them (DELETE /projects/:id) or widen this
#                   list, then restart.
#   POST /projects  a non-allowlisted repo is rejected with 400 before
#                   anything is cloned.
#
# Fails closed: WARREN_AUTH=public with this unset or empty REFUSES the
# boot — an empty list never means "allow everything".
#
# CHOOSING A SHAPE. An owner entry admits EVERY repo under that owner,
# including private ones and any repo created later. That is only safe for
# a dedicated org whose contents are uniformly public. For a personal
# account that also holds private work, list the repos explicitly — this
# is the common case and the reason repo entries exist.
#
# Neither shape asks GitHub whether a repo is really public: that would put
# a network dependency on every registration and on boot. The list is
# offline and deterministic, so it is on you to only list what you would
# publish.
# WARREN_PUBLIC_ALLOWLIST=my-org,some-owner/some-repo
#
# Deprecated alias, still read (with a warning) so an older .env keeps
# working. It accepts repo entries too — only the name is deprecated.
# WARREN_PUBLIC_ORG_ALLOWLIST=my-org

# ---------- optional: server bind ----------

# WARREN_BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0
# WARREN_BIND_PORT=8080
#
# Host-vs-container distinction (docker-compose only): WARREN_BIND_PORT
# moves the CONTAINER-INTERNAL bind. WARREN_HOST_PORT moves the HOST
# publish — the port your machine exposes — while the container side
# stays fixed at 8080. Set this when the host already has 8080 in use;
# docker-compose.yml maps "${WARREN_HOST_PORT:-8080}:8080".
# WARREN_HOST_PORT=8080
# WARREN_BIND_SOCKET=/run/warren/warren.sock      # presence flips bind to unix
# WARREN_DISABLE_UI=                                # "1" to skip serving src/ui/dist
# WARREN_UI_DIST_DIR=/app/src/ui/dist               # override UI dist path

# ---------- optional: extra seed agents (warren-e376) ----------

# Path to a JSON array of AgentDefinition objects seeded into the agents
# registry on every boot, alongside the built-ins (the registry is
# built-ins-only since pl-3a79 retired the canopy library +
# POST /agents/refresh). Idempotent and drift-aware: rows whose
# frontmatter.source is "builtin" are upserted when the file changes.
# Used by the acceptance harness to register its stub-shell agent.
# WARREN_SEED_AGENTS_FILE=/etc/warren/seed-agents.json

# ---------- optional: event-stream concurrency caps ----------

# `GET /runs/:id/events?follow=1` and `GET /plan-runs/:id/events?follow=1`
# hold a connection open for the life of a run, and warren disables Bun's
# per-request idle timeout so a quiet agent's stream isn't reaped. The
# control plane is a single replica, so these caps are what keep the two
# routes from being an unbounded connection-exhaustion vector (warren-25f6).
# Over either cap the request gets an immediate 503 + `Retry-After`;
# already-attached streams are untouched. Set a knob to 0 to disable it.
# `warren_event_streams` on /metrics reports the live count.
#
# Instance-wide concurrent event streams.
# WARREN_MAX_EVENT_STREAMS=200
#
# Concurrent event streams per client. The client is read from
# `X-Forwarded-For` counting from the RIGHT, skipping
# WARREN_EVENT_STREAM_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS infrastructure entries, else the
# socket peer address. The left-most hop is client-controlled, so keying on
# it let one source rotate the header and drain the global budget.
# WARREN_MAX_EVENT_STREAMS_PER_CLIENT=5
#
# Wall-clock budget for one connection, as a duration ("4h", "90m").
# Defaults to 4h. Expiry ends the stream cleanly and the UI treats a clean
# close as "ended" rather than reconnecting, so a client watching a run for
# longer than this stops getting live updates until it reconnects. Set 0 to
# disable the cap, at the cost of a wedged client holding a slot forever.
# WARREN_EVENT_STREAM_MAX_LIFETIME=4h

# ---------- optional: storage ----------

# Data root. The compose volume mounts here; warren.db and cloned project
# repos all live under it.
# WARREN_DATA_DIR=/data

# Database URL (R-13 bring-your-own-database). When unset, warren falls
# back to WARREN_DB_PATH; when both are unset, it defaults to a sqlite db
# under WARREN_DATA_DIR. WARREN_DB_URL wins when both are set.
#   sqlite:///data/warren.db   sqlite at /data/warren.db (default shape)
#   :memory:                   ephemeral in-memory sqlite (tests only)
#   postgres://user:pw@host/db postgres-backed control plane (org-scale)
# WARREN_DB_URL=sqlite:///data/warren.db
# WARREN_DB_POOL_MAX=10         pg pool size; sqlite ignores this.

# Legacy sqlite path. Synthesized into a `sqlite://` WARREN_DB_URL when
# WARREN_DB_URL is unset. Prefer WARREN_DB_URL going forward.
# WARREN_DB_PATH=/data/warren.db
# Clone root for project repos. Defaults to <WARREN_DATA_DIR>/projects, so
# clones stay on the one mounted volume (warren-5c42). Only override it when
# the clones belong somewhere else, and keep that path on a volume the agent
# runtime can also see.
# WARREN_PROJECTS_DIR=/data/projects

# Test substrate (R-13 step 6). Drives `withDb()` in src/db/testing.ts.
# Defaults to sqlite (today's `:memory:` path). Set both to opt into pg
# unit tests; CI exposes them in the postgres matrix job. Each `withDb()`
# call creates a fresh `warren_test_<8hex>` schema and drops it on close.
# WARREN_TEST_DIALECT=postgres
# WARREN_TEST_PG_URL=postgres://warren:warren@127.0.0.1:5432/warren_test

# ---------- optional: supervisor ----------

# Override the binary the supervisor execs. Defaults match the image
# layout (bun on PATH, server entry at src/server/main/index.ts).
# WARREN_SUPERVISOR_BUN=bun
# WARREN_SERVER_ENTRY=src/server/main.ts

# ---------- optional: tooling ----------

# WARREN_GIT_BINARY=git
# WARREN_CN_BINARY=cn
# WARREN_LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------- optional: agent git identity (warren-fe67) ----------

# Per-deployment git identity for agent commits. When both are set, the
# supervisor runs `git config --global user.name/user.email` and exports
# GIT_AUTHOR_NAME / GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL / GIT_COMMITTER_NAME / GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL
# into run processes. Unset → warren logs a warn-level fallback notice
# (warren-6a28) and agent commits use the host git identity (typically
# unconfigured in a fresh container).
#
# Recommended: create a dedicated GitHub machine account for your deployment
# (GitHub's ToS permits one bot account per user) and use its noreply email so
# agent commits attribute to the bot, not to your personal account. Find the
# account's numeric id at https://api.github.com/users/<bot-login>; the
# noreply format is `<id>+<bot-login>@users.noreply.github.com`. Pointing
# GITHUB_TOKEN at a PAT minted by the same machine account also moves PR
# authorship off your personal account (repos owned by a personal account
# need a classic repo-scoped PAT — fine-grained PATs cannot target another
# user's repos).
# WARREN_GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=your-bot-login
# WARREN_GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=1234567+your-bot-login@users.noreply.github.com

# Warren's own bookkeeping-bot identity (reap-time `chore(warren): ...`
# commits — seeds state), distinct from the agent author identity above.
# Defaults to `warren <bot@warren.invalid>` — an RFC 2606 non-routable
# address that attributes to nobody. Override BOTH (both halves or nothing)
# to attribute bookkeeping commits to your own bot account (warren-02cd),
# e.g. an address verified on the machine account.
# WARREN_BOT_NAME=your-bot-login
# WARREN_BOT_EMAIL=bot@your-domain.example

# ---------- optional: pull-request auto-open (warren-f6af) ----------

# After reap pushes the agent's branch, warren can open a GitHub PR for
# it automatically. Default ON. Set to "false" / "0" / "no" / "off" to
# disable globally. Skips silently when the run isn't successful, the
# push lands no commits, the branch matches the project default, or
# the forge has no credential (e.g. GITHUB_TOKEN unset under PAT mode).
# WARREN_AUTO_OPEN_PR=true

# Optional warren UI base URL embedded in the PR body so reviewers can
# jump back to the run that produced the change. Omit to skip the link.
# WARREN_BASE_URL=https://warren.example.com

# ---------- optional: run-branch prefix (warren-9993) ----------

# Warren composes the run workspace branch as `<prefix>/<run.id>`. The
# default is `warren` (warren-2de0 flipped the legacy `burrow` default; set
# WARREN_RUN_BRANCH_PREFIX=burrow to roll back).
#
# Per-project `.warren/defaults.json.runBranchPrefix` wins over this env
# value when both are set. Validation: kebab/snake-case (lowercase,
# digits, dots, dashes, underscores); invalid values silently fall back to
# the next precedence slot — they never block a spawn.
# WARREN_RUN_BRANCH_PREFIX=warren

# ---------- optional: per-run preview environments (R-19 / docs/design/preview-environments.md) ----------

# Operator's preview host suffix for subdomain mode. When set with
# WARREN_PREVIEW_MODE=subdomain, warren routes
# `Host: run-<runId>.<WARREN_PREVIEW_HOST>` to the in-sandbox preview port.
# TLS termination, wildcard DNS, and wildcard certificate provisioning stay
# on the operator's edge. Default path mode does not require this value: it
# derives the host from the incoming warren request and serves previews on
# the dedicated preview listener.
# WARREN_PREVIEW_HOST=preview.warren.example.com

# Preview routing mode (warren-fcb7 / docs/design/preview-environments.md path-mode addendum). The
# default `path` reuses the single hostname + cert that already serves the
# warren UI; previews are at `https://<warren-host>/p/<run-id>/` and no
# wildcard DNS / cert is required — the right shape for a solo-operator
# single-box deploy. Set to `subdomain` for the original docs/design/preview-environments.md behavior
# (`https://run-<id>.<WARREN_PREVIEW_HOST>/`), which scales to many
# concurrent previews per host but requires a wildcard CNAME +
# DNS-01-issued wildcard cert. A per-project `.warren/preview.yaml` `mode:`
# pin is overridden by this env value when both are set.
# WARREN_PREVIEW_MODE=path

# Dedicated path-mode preview listener port (warren-3f8a). Path-mode
# previews are served from their own listener — same hostname, this port —
# so the preview gets its own browser origin and agent-authored preview
# code cannot read the operator token out of the warren UI's localStorage.
# The warren origin answers `/p/<run-id>/` with a 308 to this origin.
# Publish this port next to the API port (docker-compose maps
# "${WARREN_HOST_PREVIEW_PORT:-8081}:8081"; a reverse proxy needs a
# second forwarded port on the same hostname).
#
# Host-vs-container distinction (docker-compose only):
# WARREN_PREVIEW_PORT moves the CONTAINER-INTERNAL preview listener.
# WARREN_HOST_PREVIEW_PORT moves the HOST publish — the port your
# machine exposes — while the container side stays fixed at 8081.
# WARREN_HOST_PREVIEW_PORT=8081
# Default: the bind port + 1. Ignored in subdomain mode and on the
# unix-socket transport (which keeps the legacy same-origin mounting and
# warns at boot).
# WARREN_PREVIEW_PORT=8081

# Port range the SQLite-backed allocator picks from when reap launches a
# preview sidecar. In-use ports are derived from
# `runs.preview_state IN ('starting','live')` so the allocator is restart-
# safe; exhaustion emits a `preview_failed` event with `reason=port_exhausted`
# and `warren doctor` warns when ≥80% of the range is in use. Default
# `30000-31000`.
# WARREN_PREVIEW_PORT_RANGE=30000-31000

# Eviction-worker tuning. The sweep combines three signals + manual teardown:
#   (1) idle TTL — primary kill-rule, measured from proxy-tracked
#       `preview_last_hit_at` (falls back to `preview_started_at` for
#       launched-but-never-visited previews).
#   (2) max_lifetime — hard ceiling from launch time, regardless of activity.
#   (3) global LRU cap — bounds container memory; longest-idle wins.
# Per-project preview.idle_ttl / preview.max_lifetime in
# `.warren/defaults.json` override the global env values at tick time.
# Duration strings accept `s` / `m` / `h` (e.g. `30m`, `1h30m`).
# WARREN_PREVIEW_IDLE_TTL=30m
# WARREN_PREVIEW_MAX_LIFETIME=8h
# WARREN_PREVIEW_MAX_LIVE=20
# WARREN_PREVIEW_EVICTION_TICK_MS=60000

# Escape hatch — disable the eviction worker entirely. Intended for
# acceptance tests that drive eviction inline; do not set in production.
# WARREN_PREVIEW_EVICTION_DISABLED=

# Fallback GC for stranded run workspaces (warren-0a9a). Per-reap
# workspace teardown covers the happy path; this periodic sweep reclaims
# workspaces stranded by a warren crash mid-reap or a run force-killed
# before reap ran. It only destroys workspaces whose runs are all terminal
# and whose newest activity is older than the TTL. The same predicate backs
# the `stale_sandbox_workspaces` check in `warren doctor` and `/readyz`.
# Duration strings accept `s` / `m` / `h` (e.g. `1h`, `30m`).
# WARREN_WORKSPACE_GC_TTL=1h
# WARREN_WORKSPACE_GC_TICK_MS=300000
# Escape hatch — disable the GC sweep entirely (the doctor/readyz check
# still reports stranded workspaces so disk leaks stay visible).
# WARREN_WORKSPACE_GC_DISABLED=

# Run heartbeat watchdog (warren-285d). Force-fails a `running` run that
# goes silent-but-busy — e.g. a runaway quality-gate command behind a
# stuck bash tool call (biome pinning cores, exitCode null, no new
# events). When a run's newest event (or `started_at` fallback) is older
# than this budget, the watchdog cancels the runtime workload and reaps it
# `failed` (failure_reason=timed_out) so its workspace and process tree are
# torn down instead of pinning CPU forever.
#
# On by default (warren-b2dc) with a generous built-in budget (45 min =
# 2700000ms) — well above any legitimately slow-but-silent tool (cold
# `bun install`, large clone) — so a fresh deploy is protected without an
# explicit env var. Override the budget (milliseconds) here:
# WARREN_RUN_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000
# Opt out entirely (or pin the budget to 0):
# WARREN_WATCHDOG_DISABLED=1
# Tick cadence for the watchdog sweep (default 30000 = 30s).
# WARREN_WATCHDOG_TICK_MS=30000

# ---------- optional: background coordinators & model tiers ----------

# Background coordinator + model-tier knobs surfaced here for completeness.
# Each defaults to a safe value (stated per var); set only to tune or disable
# a specific worker. Truthy values throughout: 1 / true / yes / on
# (case-insensitive).

# (a) Seeds CLI binary override. The trigger scheduler shells out to the
# seeds CLI (`sd`) to sync issues; the plan-run / project handlers share the
# same binary and recover a missing-`sd` boot with the hint "set
# WARREN_SD_BINARY (or install sd on PATH) and restart". Default resolves `sd`
# from PATH. An empty string is rejected at config load (ValidationError) —
# unset to fall back to the default.
# WARREN_SD_BINARY=sd

# (b) Plan-run coordinator merge-wait budget (warren-3937). Bounded
# wall-clock budget (ms) for a PR to merge once its run has ended — guards
# the parent-merge gate and the child waiting_for_merge branch against a PR
# that is open-but-unmergeable (failing checks, BLOCKED merge state, stuck
# auto-merge). Default 30 min = 1800000ms; set to 0 to disable the timeout
# (unbounded wait).
# WARREN_PLAN_RUN_MERGE_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000

# (c) Pause/respawn detector. The pause detector is OFF by default; set to 1
# to opt in (it watches for runs that pause abnormally). Polls every
# WARREN_PAUSE_DETECTOR_TICK_MS (default 15s).
# WARREN_PAUSE_DETECTOR_ENABLED=1

# (d) Model-tier overrides for the built-in agents (healer, pi, pr-fixer).
# The literal model identifiers live only in src/registry/builtins/model-tiers.ts,
# so bumping a model is a single edit
# or a deploy-time env override with no image rebuild. Resolution freezes
# onto each agent row at seed time (seedBuiltinAgents), so set these before
# warren boots. Defaults: sonnet = claude-sonnet-4-6 / anthropic,
# opus = claude-opus-4-8 / anthropic.
# WARREN_MODEL_SONNET=claude-sonnet-4-6
# WARREN_MODEL_SONNET_PROVIDER=anthropic
# WARREN_MODEL_OPUS=claude-opus-4-8
# WARREN_MODEL_OPUS_PROVIDER=anthropic
